DriveTests - stress tests for Amiga disk drivers and file systems
==================================================================

Four tools that look at a drive from three levels:

  flysuite   the DRIVER: it drives a unit of any trackdisk-style device
             driver (CMD_READ, TD64, NSD, HD_SCSICMD) and checks every
             answer. Written for an NCR 53C80 SCSI driver, usable on any
             driver: DEVICE= and a unit.
  devsoak    the DRIVER again, DESTRUCTIVELY: Simon Dick's soak tester
             overwrites every sector of a range or a partition you give
             it, keeps per-sector state, and proves the CONTENT stays
             right for hours with several tasks and several requests in
             flight (plus the edge cases: bounds, alignment, zero length,
             the 64-bit high word, stale change counts). Where flysuite
             saves and restores, devsoak destroys: give it a partition
             you do not need. Kickstart 1.3 and up.
  AmiFSX     the FILES: a port of fsx, the file system exerciser from
             NeXT/Apple still used by FreeBSD and xfstests. It keeps a
             model of a test file and hammers the real one with random
             reads, writes, size changes and reopens, checking every byte.
  AmiDefrag  the STRUCTURES ON DISK: its analyse mode walks the file
             system's own bitmap, directories and file headers (FFS,
             PFS3, SFS) and maps every block - free, data, system,
             unknown. Run it after the other two: damage that neither a
             driver check nor a file check can see (a cross-linked block,
             a bitmap out of step) shows up here. Its block layer is
             read-only until the defragmenter is deliberately armed; this
             kit only recommends the analyse pass.

A driver bug then shows up at the level it lives at: a wrong error code
or a dropped byte in flysuite, a corrupted file in AmiFSX, a damaged
structure in AmiDefrag; devsoak catches a driver that silently corrupts
data under load. All need AmigaOS 2.04 or newer except devsoak (1.3);
flysuite, AmiFSX and devsoak run on a 68000.

In this drawer
  flysuite        the driver test (flysuite.md: every option and check)
  AmiFSX          the file system exerciser (AmiFSX.md)
  AmiDefrag       the block-map analyser (AmiDefrag.md) - version 0.3,
                  by John Hertell (director) and Claude Code (code);
                  the defrag engines are beta, the analyse mode is what
                  you want here. NOTE: in our tests (OS 3.2.3, FFS, a
                  real A1200 and WinUAE) 0.3 printed its map in full and
                  then REBOOTED the machine on exit. RunTests therefore
                  skips it unless you add AMIDEFRAG; run it by hand on a
                  machine with nothing unsaved. Reported upstream.
  devsoak         the destructive soak tester (devsoak.md: the whole
                  manual) - version 1.1, by Simon Dick (sidick). It is
                  NOT run by RunTests: it overwrites the partition or
                  range you name, with no undo. By hand only.
  devsoak.quirks  its list of known driver-specific behaviour; devsoak
                  looks for it beside its own binary, so keep them together
  RAD-FFS         a mountlist for a RAD: FFS volume, a harmless first
                  target for AmiFSX
  LICENSE         GPL-3.0 for flysuite and AmiFSX (github.com/lainejones)
  LICENSE-AmiDefrag  MIT (github.com/ChuckyGang/AmiDefrag)
  LICENSE-devsoak    BSD 2-Clause (github.com/sidick/devsoak)


Running it
----------
From Workbench: double-click RunTests. It asks for a SCRATCH drawer on
the drive to test (a file requester), works out the drive's device driver
and unit from it, then asks Quick or Full:
  Quick  reads only, writes nothing: flysuite's conf/big/edge/dma16/
         latency/audit
  Full   also writes test files in that drawer (made, checked and deleted
         again) and runs AmiFSX there
(the AmiDefrag analysis only with AMIDEFRAG on the Shell line - see above)
The output shows in its window, every log is saved in the drawer
(flysuite.log, amifsx.log, amidefrag.log), and a requester at the end
says how it went. From a Shell the same, from inside this drawer:
  CD Work:DriveTests
  Execute RunTests Work:tests Full
with the answers given (left out, it asks). A SCSI bus with a network
adapter on it (a DaynaPORT emulation on a BlueSCSI/ZuluSCSI, say) needs
that id kept out of the probes - flysuite's absent-id checks would send
it INQUIRY and TEST UNIT READY while the network driver is using it:
  Execute RunTests Work:tests Quick SKIPIDS=5
(from the icon the script asks for no ids; use the Shell form then). The tools can also be run one by one (below).

From a PC: these run on the Amiga, not on the PC. Get the drawer onto the
Amiga whole (LhA on the Amiga keeps the executable bits; unpacked on a
PC and copied over, the bits are lost - RunTests puts them back, a Shell
user does `Protect flysuite +e` and the same for AmiFSX, AmiDefrag,
devsoak and volunit). In an emulator (WinUAE, Copperline) with the drawer in a host
folder it runs the same way, against the emulated drive - fine for a
driver that runs in the emulator, no use for one that only exists on
real hardware.

If it crashes
-------------
flysuite itself does not freeze a machine: every wait is bounded and a
request that never comes back is reported as HANG. A driver can still
take the machine down (a Guru, or a reset with nothing on screen). Then:
  - reboot; a volume that was being written validates itself (FFS shows
    it busy for a while) - that is normal
  - read flysuite.log in the scratch drawer: it is written line by line,
    so the last line names the test that was running when the machine
    went down. That line is the report.
  - Full mode leaves test files behind (flysuite.* and fsxtest* in the
    scratch drawer): delete them.
  - a RAWWRITE range (an option you gave by hand; RunTests uses none)
    keeps its saved copy in RAM, so after a crash the range holds test
    data. The tool only accepts a range outside every partition and
    mounted volume, so that is unused space, not your files.
  - AmiDefrag's analysis and AmiFSX in Quick mode write nothing.
  - devsoak (by hand only) had overwritten the partition you gave it
    before anything crashed: that partition is scrap either way.
A crash in Quick mode is a driver finding in itself: say which test.


The tools in order, on a scratch volume (by hand)
-------------------------------------------------
  flysuite DEVICE=yourscsi.device UNIT=0 TESTS=all WRITEFILE=Scratch:t
  AmiFSX Scratch:fsxtest N=100000 P=5000 C=100 XREAD
  AmiDefrag Scratch: MAP WIDTH=72

and read the AmiDefrag map for anything "unknown". Then, if the scratch
partition can go:

  devsoak Scratch: -d -t 30m -y

devsoak takes the partition's own extent as its range (nothing outside
it is written), inhibits the file system for the run and overwrites
every sector in it, with several tasks at once, for the time you give
it (-t 8h for a real soak; -w and -q for more workers and deeper
queues - give those a longer watchdog, -W 60 for -w 4 -q 4 on a slow
drive). A WATCHDOG FAIL ends devsoak but leaves its worker tasks
running: reboot straight after one. A partition with a mounted volume
on it must be confirmed by typing the volume's name back; -y does not
skip that. Reformat the partition afterwards. devsoak.md has every option.


flysuite - first run, read-only
-------------------------------
  flysuite DEVICE=yourscsi.device UNIT=0 TESTS=conf,big,edge,audit

Nothing is written. Every wait is bounded: a request that never comes
back is reported as HANG with the command that caused it, and the run
ends with a summary instead of a frozen machine. Return code 0 = all
passed, 5 = a FAIL, 10 = a HANG, 20 = refused.

Writing needs an explicit option:
  WRITEFILE=Scratch:dir     file writes in that drawer (made, verified,
                            deleted again)
  RAWWRITE=first,count      raw block writes ONLY in that range (max 1 MB);
                            the blocks are saved first and restored at
                            the end and on every error path. A range over
                            an RDB, a partition or a mounted volume is
                            refused.

The groups (TESTS=): conf (HD_SCSICMD conformance), big (1-2048 block
reads through every path, across 2/4 GB), edge (end of unit, odd
lengths, the trackdisk queries, open/close), dma16 (reads into RAM
above 16 MB, which a Zorro II DMA controller can't reach: the driver
must copy), latency (does a priority-21 task keep running while the
driver reads - catches Forbid/Disable around transfers), conc (several
tasks at once + AbortIO), soak (MINUTES= of write/read/compare), mix (every
SCSI/IDE read+write combination at once, for two-port drivers), audit
(one test per finding of a 53C80 driver audit: error codes, data
direction mismatches, byte-1 rewriting, first-open race, ATAPI
direction), fuzz (random non-write CDBs, emulator only). `all` = what is
safe without extra options. Two tests can wedge a machine and need DANGER
(ATAPIDIR, RUNEND): emulator, own boot.

What the audit tests look for in a driver:
  ERRMAP   reads past the end fail with a seek / not-specified error,
           not "bad sector" or "write protected"
  DIRMIS   a data phase against the caller's direction flag, or past
           its buffer, must not invent bytes: no zeros to the disk,
           scsi_Actual = bytes really moved. Run this one: a 53C80 phase
           loop gets it wrong easily, ours did.
  CDBPASS  byte 1 of a LUN 0 command reaches the target untouched
  RACEOPEN several first OpenDevice calls at once share one unit
  ATAPIDIR an ATAPI command without SCSIF_READ must not loop forever

A FAIL is the driver's; "the TARGET sends less than its header says"
is the drive's and counts as a pass.


AmiFSX - first run
------------------
  Mount RAD: FROM RAD-FFS        (a 2 MB FFS volume in RAM, optional)
  AmiFSX RAD:fsxtest N=20000 P=1000

then on the drive under test:

  AmiFSX DH1:fsxtest N=100000 P=5000 C=100 XREAD

N= operations (0 = forever, Ctrl-C stops), P= progress every N ops,
C= close/reopen 1 in N ops, XREAD = a second handle for half the reads
(cache coherency), CHIP = buffers in chip RAM (DMA paths), STRICT =
extended space must read as zeroes. On the first mismatch it dumps the
last 1024 operations and keeps the expected content next to the file.
Return code 0 passed, 10 failure. Same SEED = same run.


With an AI
----------
Give Claude (claude.ai, or Claude Code in your driver's repository) the
flysuite.log and your driver source and ask what failed and why: one line
per check with the command and the expected/got values is what it needs,
and flysuite.md explains every check. The repository
github.com/lainejones/DriveTests has a CLAUDE.md for coding agents: what
the tools are, the order to run them, where to put the log, how to read a
FAIL, a HANG or a crash, and to reproduce before fixing.


Reporting
---------
flysuite: the LOG=file (every line, flushed as it goes) and the summary;
SEED= repeats a run. AmiFSX: the operation dump and the seed.
Issues: github.com/lainejones/DriveTests (the kit, RunTests, volunit),
github.com/lainejones/DataFlyerSCSI (flysuite),
github.com/lainejones/AmiFSX, github.com/sidick/devsoak and
github.com/ChuckyGang/AmiDefrag.


